"help.windowTitle" = "AI Usage Help";
"help.title" = "About stale data";
"help.intro" = "Stale means AI Usage cannot confirm that the displayed values are current. It can appear after collection fails, when the observation time is missing or invalid, or when the last valid observation is more than six hours old.";
"help.warning" = "The badge alone does not show that a quota was exceeded or an account is invalid. Usage and reset times shown while stale may no longer be current.";
"help.collectionNote.format" = "Last collection note: %@";
"help.observed.format" = "Observed: %@";
"help.lastChecked.format" = "Last checked: %@";
"help.checkedExplanation" = "“Last checked” is when AI Usage attempted a refresh. A recent check can remain stale if no new valid values were obtained.";
"help.steps" = "What to try";
"help.claude.title" = "Claude Code";
"help.claude.body" = "Usage comes from the credentials terminal Claude Code stores in the Keychain. The desktop app alone never writes them, so run claude in Terminal and sign in once. AI Usage reads them through /usr/bin/security, the same tool Claude Code uses, so there is normally no Keychain prompt. If a prompt naming security appears and you decline it, AI Usage waits until you choose Check Again before asking again. When terminal Claude Code is installed, AI Usage only reads those credentials and leaves renewing them to the CLI, so when it reports expired credentials, run claude in Terminal. Without the terminal CLI installed, AI Usage renews them itself. Also check your network. For the statusLine source, complete a normal response in terminal Claude Code. Wait if rate-limited.";
"help.codex.title" = "Codex";
"help.codex.body" = "Usage comes from the official Codex client. AI Usage runs codex app-server and reads the same values /status shows, without running a model or writing anything to ~/.codex. It looks for codex where command line tools are usually installed and inside ChatGPT.app, so installing either the CLI or the official app is enough. Without one of them it falls back to the local session log, which only terminal Codex updates: complete a normal Codex response, then check again. Checking again does not update that log by itself.";
"help.grok.title" = "Grok";
"help.grok.body" = "Use Grok normally so it can update its local billing log, then check again.";
"help.widget.body" = "Opening the widget alone does not collect data. Open the AI Usage app and check again. The medium widget shows four quota rows. An agent with more than one window uses more than one row, so roughly three agents fit. It fills those rows in card order, so reorder the cards in Settings to change which agents appear.";
"help.checkAgain.detail" = "Checking again retries collection, but it cannot force Grok to write new logs or bypass a provider's rate limit.";
"help.privacy" = "For support, do not share OAuth tokens, Keychain contents, complete session logs, or raw statusLine logs.";

